Exploring a method
Each method has one hyperparameter. Switch genotypes on and off to compare their coefficients (top); the lower panel shows the held-out error for all settings, as the mean over 20 genotypes.

d3 = window.d3
Plot = window.Plot
hyper = FileAttachment("data/interactive/hyper.json").json()
contrast = ["g181", "g171", "g021", "g141", "g151"]
shades = ["#8B1E3F", "#014E81", "#00A6D6", "#6D8B1E", "#6B7280"]
viewof method = select("Method", Object.keys(hyper.methods), "STLSQ")
spec = hyper.methods[method]
viewof shown = toggles("Genotypes", contrast, shades)
viewof step = slider(spec.param, spec.values, Math.floor(spec.values.length / 2), v =&gt; (+v).toPrecision(2))

































































{
  const all = Object.values(hyper.methods);
  const reach = d3.max(all.flatMap(m =&gt; m.effect.flat(2).map(Math.abs)));
  const rmse = [d3.min(all.flatMap(m =&gt; m.rmse)), 0.8];   // cap: very large penalties are off scale
  const band = 6, n = shown.length;
  const key = (term, name) =&gt; term + "|" + name;
  const domain = hyper.terms.flatMap(term =&gt; [...shown.map(name =&gt; key(term, name)), key(term, "")]);
  const middle = hyper.terms.map(term =&gt; key(term, shown[Math.floor(n / 2)]));
  const rows = shown.flatMap(name =&gt; {
    const g = hyper.genotypes.indexOf(name);
    return hyper.terms.map((term, i) =&gt; ({name, band: key(term, name), effect: spec.effect[step][g][i]}));
  });
  const coefficients = Plot.plot({
    width: 520, height: 40 + band * domain.length, marginLeft: 56,
    x: {domain: [-reach, reach], label: "scaled coefficient", ticks: 4},
    y: {domain, ticks: middle, tickFormat: d =&gt; d.split("|")[0], label: null, padding: 0},
    color: {domain: contrast, range: shades},
    marks: [Plot.barX(rows, {x: "effect", y: "band", fill: "name"}), Plot.ruleX([0])]
  });
  const curve = spec.values.map((value, i) =&gt; ({value, rmse: spec.rmse[i]}));
  const scale = spec.param === "terms" ? "linear" : "log";
  const error = Plot.plot({
    width: 520, height: 220,
    x: {type: scale, label: spec.param, tickFormat: ".2~g"},
    y: {domain: [rmse[0] - 0.01, rmse[1] + 0.01], label: "held-out RMSE (t/ha)"},
    marks: [Plot.line(curve, {x: "value", y: "rmse", stroke: "#9CA3AF", clip: true}),
            Plot.dot(curve, {x: "value", y: "rmse", fill: "#9CA3AF", r: 3, clip: true}),
            Plot.dot([curve[step]], {x: "value", y: "rmse", fill: "#8B1E3F", r: 6, clip: true})]
  });
  return html`&lt;div&gt;${coefficients}${error}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}







shown.length ? html`&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;p class="interactive-note"&gt;Fitted equation for genotype ${shown[0]} (${method}, ${spec.param} ${(+spec.values[step]).toPrecision(2)}); the terms are functions of the standardised drivers.&lt;/p&gt;
  ${Equation.element(Equation.latex(hyper.terms, spec.intercept[step][hyper.genotypes.indexOf(shown[0])], spec.coef[step][hyper.genotypes.indexOf(shown[0])]))}&lt;/div&gt;` : html``

Choose the modelled states, the drivers, the library and the fitting method, and see the resulting equations and their prediction for years that were left out of the fit. The calculations run in the browser and follow the functions of the previous steps: each genotype is fitted to all years for the coefficients, and leave-one-year-out validation gives the held-out error.
With one modelled state \(y\) (biomass or LAI) the model is \(\dot{y} = \boldsymbol{\xi}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\theta}(\mathbf{u})\) with weather drivers \(\mathbf{u}\). With state feedback the modelled states enter the library as well, \(\dot{y}_k = \boldsymbol{\xi}_k^{\top}\boldsymbol{\theta}(\mathbf{u}, y_1, y_2)\), and with both states modelled the two equations form a coupled system with a coefficient vector \(\boldsymbol{\xi}_k\) each. States are standardised like the drivers, and with products the library also contains state × state and state × driver terms. The coupled system is integrated over the held-out year; a year whose solution grows beyond ten times the largest observed value counts as diverged and is left out of the error. In our tests, feedback was typically not better than weather-only models and can be unstable.
